---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { skanpayDefinitions } from "../content/site";
---
<BaseLayout
  title="SkanPay Infrastructure"
  description="SkanPay is the QR checkout infrastructure direction behind BeamCash, focused on request creation, buyer context, partner routing, redemption coordination, and seller status."
  activePath="/qr-payments/"
>
  <section class="tech-hero">
    <div class="tech-hero-inner">
      <div class="tech-hero-copy motion-item">
        <p class="eyebrow">SkanPay</p>
        <h1>SkanPay turns a QR scan into a checkout request.</h1>
        <p>
          BeamCash shows the experience. SkanPay is the patent-pending infrastructure model behind it: request creation, buyer context, partner routing, redemption context, and seller-readable status.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="request-object motion-item" aria-label="Example QR checkout request fields">
        <div><span>request.id</span><strong>sp_req_2048</strong></div>
        <div><span>merchant</span><strong>BeamCash Demo Stall</strong></div>
        <div><span>amount</span><strong>buyer reviewed</strong></div>
        <div><span>state</span><strong>awaiting decision</strong></div>
        <div><span>result</span><strong>seller-readable status</strong></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="definition-section">
    <div class="definition-inner">
      <div class="tech-section-heading motion-item">
        <p class="eyebrow">Definition</p>
        <h2>Keep the boundary clear.</h2>
        <p>SkanPay should be understood as the system layer around the checkout request, not as the BeamCash app or a replacement for every payment system.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="definition-lines" aria-label="What SkanPay is and is not">
        {skanpayDefinitions.map((group) => (
          <article class="motion-item">
            <h3>{group.label}</h3>
            <ul>
              {group.items.map((item) => (
                <li>{item}</li>
              ))}
            </ul>
          </article>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="state-machine">
    <div class="state-machine-inner">
      <div class="tech-section-heading motion-item">
        <p class="eyebrow">State model</p>
        <h2>The request has to move through clear states.</h2>
        <p>A scan is the start of the checkout request, not the whole payment experience.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="state-rail" aria-label="SkanPay checkout state model">
        <article class="motion-item">
          <span>Created</span>
          <strong>Seller starts request</strong>
          <p>Amount, seller reference, session details, and optional redemption data are prepared.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="motion-item">
          <span>Scanned</span>
          <strong>Buyer opens request</strong>
          <p>The buyer sees seller and amount context before choosing an approval path.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="motion-item">
          <span>Decided</span>
          <strong>Partner returns decision</strong>
          <p>The request can be approved, cancelled, expired, failed, or remain pending.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="motion-item">
          <span>Confirmed</span>
          <strong>Seller receives status</strong>
          <p>The merchant or seller gets a readable result before closing the sale.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="protocol-split">
    <div class="protocol-split-inner">
      <div class="protocol-copy motion-item">
        <p class="eyebrow">Boundary</p>
        <h2>Define the boundary between checkout requests and payment systems.</h2>
        <p>
          SkanPay sits around the request and state layer. Processors, banks, wallets, and offer systems stay explicit where they authorize money movement or validate value.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="boundary-map motion-item" aria-label="Gateway and partner responsibilities">
        <div>
          <span>SkanPay</span>
          <strong>Request + state layer</strong>
          <p>Structures the checkout context and status result.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <span>Processor / bank</span>
          <strong>Money movement</strong>
          <p>Handles authorization, clearing, and settlement where applicable.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <span>Offer system</span>
          <strong>Redemption logic</strong>
          <p>Coordinates coupon, voucher, loyalty, or stored-value decisions.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="integration-runway">
    <div class="integration-runway-inner">
      <div class="tech-section-heading motion-item">
        <p class="eyebrow">Integration touchpoints</p>
        <h2>What partners can evaluate.</h2>
        <p>Public details should stay focused. Partner-specific requirements belong in direct review.</p>
      </div>

      <ol class="runway-list">
        <li class="motion-item"><span>01</span><strong>QR request format</strong></li>
        <li class="motion-item"><span>02</span><strong>Supported app or wallet behavior</strong></li>
        <li class="motion-item"><span>03</span><strong>Status and timeout handling</strong></li>
        <li class="motion-item"><span>04</span><strong>Coupon or voucher validation</strong></li>
        <li class="motion-item"><span>05</span><strong>Merchant confirmation display</strong></li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
